summary:
First technical review of phase E: submission sub-mv3vs3h1-0181fd20 (76 artifacts), iteration 1 of 3. I bound the review to the artifacts by the sha256 recorded in the submission: all 76 match, and nothing under src/, test/, public/, scripts/ or package.json changed between 5e1895b and HEAD 38145b5 (records only). All my test runs carry the submission's tree digest 5f628506...53603.

What I read:
- submission.md, handoff.json, and all eleven e0-e6 handoffs;
- the plan's phase E tasks and criteria;
- the spec's payment sections: port and Outcome, wire mapping, 004/005, listener, transaction discipline, provider call protocol and sender table, return handler, void rule, saga, reconciler, webhooks, execute check order;
- followups.md (phase E section, FU-33..FU-40);
- the cited decisions plus dec-mv3vui7s-014f6e7c, dec-mv3vuigy-0109feb6 and dec-mv3vuiqw-01d3c5c9;
- the code of calls.js, payments.js, void-rule.js, saga.js, reconciler.js, webhooks.js, fake.js, sandbox.js (call path), db.tx, app.js wiring and the execute route;
- the security-reviewer's FINAL draft phase-e-security-1.md, folded in below.

Safety: every command ran with PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin. I never set RS_LIVE_MODEL or RS_LIVE_PAYPAL and never ran the live PayPal suite. No PayPal host was contacted (net guard on every suite run; payments only against the fake or the loopback stub). Every mutant and probe ran in a scratch copy; the reviewed tree was never written.

My runs after the submission, all green unless stated:
- npm test: 2087 tests, 2079 pass, 0 fail, 8 skipped (the 7 fake-harness OAuth/wire scenarios and fake x PROVIDER_BUSY), 0 todo. ECCODE_EVAL {"passed":200,"total":200}. NFR5 p95 62.8 ms over 5227 requests (ev:ev-mv3vxrpc-0149549b). The RS-39 uploads-retention timer test passed, so no re-run under dec-mv3tqbn4-01d43152 was needed; it also passed in the e6 run.
- npm run test:browser: 10/10, including RS-31(a) at 360 and 1280 px, with the log line's userId 9 at both widths (ev:ev-mv3vyrjf-019df6a9).
- Plan commands, verbatim:
  - e0: 160/160 (ev:ev-mv3vzw0q-0181f487);
  - e1: 487 tests, 480 pass, 7 skipped by design (ev:ev-mv3w02vw-01d44e58);
  - e2: 92/92 (ev:ev-mv3w0fej-015bce20);
  - e3: 96/96 (ev:ev-mv3w0hhh-014c66ac);
  - e4: 71/71 (ev:ev-mv3w0x9f-015d4e13);
  - e5: 166/166 (ev:ev-mv3w0y8o-01eb4845);
  - e6: npm test 2087/2079/0 fail/8 skipped with p95 63.0 ms, then the browser suite 10/10 (ev:ev-mv3w37b9-011bb315).
- Artifact coverage: 74 declared = 74 changed in owned paths = 74 submitted; 0 undeclared, 0 outside ownership (ev:ev-mv3w3fse-015bbcde).
- TR-R4-3 probe: db.tx refuses an async function (TX_ASYNC), and 0 of 2019 provider-method entries happened inside an open transaction, across the unit+api+integration suite and the browser journey, with a positive control (ev:ev-mv3wykva-018b0000). An earlier run of the same probe failed only because my copy lacked .gitignore (ev:ev-mv3wr9gl-01927595, not cited for any criterion).
- 19 mutants of my own on the money paths. 17 are killed and both controls are green (ev:ev-mv3xewar-0170b19e). The first full run (ev:ev-mv3wo4q9-014c6847) fails by design because two mutants survive (M2, M6, see TR-E-4 and TR-E-5); it is cited only in those findings.
- Probe P1/P2 (ev:ev-mv3wp57z-01f9ce7d, also ev:ev-mv3wopms-010bc400). It passes when it OBSERVES the defect of TR-E-1, so it is cited only there.

Decision: changes requested, because of one major finding.

TR-E-1 (= SEC-E-1, major): a validly signed but late PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED moves capture_pending, or unknown(prev authorized), back to authorized. The spec maps that event pending -> authorized only. Reproduced independently by me and by security:
- a purchase whose capture is merely pending is compensated, so the buyer is charged and refunded and the purchase is lost;
- an unknown capture is re-sent without a successful read.
Money was neither lost nor doubled, because the PayPal-Request-Id replay held.

Why changes requested now rather than an approval with a routed follow-up: the phase is not approved yet, so the orchestrator can reset e3 (webhooks.js) and e2 inside this phase. After an approval no remaining task owns webhooks.js and the rework limit is used. The fix is small and test-shaped.

Also to fix in the same round:
- TR-E-2 (= SEC-E-2, minor): the public return route is not rate-limited per user and costs one provider read per hit. It rides the e2 reset.
- TR-E-3 (minor): the shared approval round-trip helper accepts a log line without userId. Owner: e6 if it is reset, else f4.

Info: TR-E-4 to TR-E-8 (two mutation survivors, contract deltas incl. four unlisted ones, the security info items SEC-E-I1..I5, TR-R4-3 confirmed).

Everything else I checked holds:
- the call protocol's queue/start/record CAS and fences;
- PayPal-Request-Id = operation key on every mutating call;
- read-before-resend on every reconciler path;
- a claim with no provider call in its transaction;
- the pause and takeover exclusion (F-TR-7);
- the reconciler tick order;
- the void rule and its hooks;
- webhook dedupe among valid rows;
- the two-process RS-20 test, which bites on a second capture;
- the cross-site journey, whose CSP assertion bites.

Decisions judged:
- FU-34 (dec-mv3vuigy-0109feb6): accepted. The backstop is tested (reconciler.test.js L385), and security's P-FU34 shows no loss or doubling in the window.
- FU-35 (dec-mv3vui7s-014f6e7c): accepted. The drain uses the pre-increment failed_drains, which matches the spec's formula.
- Four security residuals (dec-mv3vuiqw-01d3c5c9): unchanged by this phase.
- dec-mv3abkne-01161cf3, dec-mv3abkv0-011839e9, dec-mv3uqze6-0196f887 and dec-mv38g19z-01f0a99f: accepted.

criteria.notes:
The e3 plan command passes 96/96.

These parts are sound:
- the pre-checks, the per-IP limit and the byte-exact verification;
- dedupe only among valid rows: M15 (dedupe over all rows) and M16 (pre-verify dedupe removed) are killed;
- the hook in the same transaction: M14 is killed.

Not met: the acceptance criterion 'transitions only as states.js allows (CAS), else flagged', read with the spec's mapping 'PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED (pending -> authorized)'. The event has no `from` list (L54), so capture_pending and unknown(prev authorized|capture_pending) are moved to authorized. The mapping table (L521-L527) has no row for either. See TR-E-1.

The e4 plan command passes 71/71.

The claim (saga.js L137-L209) runs inside one db.tx. db.tx refuses a promise (TX_ASYNC, connection.js L94-L97), so no provider call can be awaited in it. It follows the spec's check order on stored data, then makes the conditional approved -> executing UPDATE with epoch + 1, consumes the reservation and queues capture:1 per order. The AUTHORIZATION_EXPIRED refusal commits the void rule.

Captures:
- start under G(now), with the epoch stamped on the call;
- record fenced by the epoch and the send_token;
- AUTHORIZATION_ALREADY_CAPTURED turns the call unknown and pauses, resolved by reading, never guessed;
- unknown and PENDING pause.

Reconciler: the tick order is 1-9 as in the spec (L563-L587).
- Step 2 reads before every re-send on all four kinds.
- Takeover excludes plans with capture_pending/unknown operations or a backing-off capture.
- Housekeeping purges sessions, login failures, idempotency keys, 7-day invalid/unverified webhook rows and stale model-spend rows. Rate events are purged by retention.js hourly instead: unlisted delta, TR-E-6.

Mutants killed:
- M2b (capture amount +1, 31 red);
- M3 (result fence removed);
- M4 (lease guard ignores lease_until);
- M7 (takeover ignores pending/unknown);
- M8 (unknown capture with CAPTURED read treated as CREATED);
- M9 (claim ignores AUTHORIZATION_PENDING);
- M10 (expiry refusal without the void rule).

M6 (no pause at L531) survives: TR-E-4.

The resend-without-read path of SEC-E-1(b) needs e3's defect to occur. f2 should add the step-6 read as defence in depth (TR-E-4).

Killed: M3, M4, M5 and M8, and M17 for RS-20.

Every re-send in the reconciler follows a read:
- authorize: getOrder, then APPROVED without an authorization;
- capture: CREATED, and only the lease holder re-sends;
- void: CREATED or PENDING;
- refund: a COMPLETED capture without a refund.
Each re-send uses the same key through the start CAS on unknown/send_token NULL, so two reconcilers cannot both send.

The only gap is TR-E-1's path, where a webhook clears unknown without resolving the call.

Confirmed in three ways.

Statically: every provider-method call site in src/ outside src/payments is an awaited call in an async function, outside any db.tx callback:
- calls.js L370, L405 and L410;
- payments.js L125 and L200;
- reconciler.js L141-L333;
- saga.js L424 and L468;
- webhooks.js L342.

Dynamically: in an instrumented copy, a positive control marks a provider call made inside db.tx, and db.tx(async) throws TX_ASYNC. Across the unit+api+integration suite (2000 tests, 0 fail) and the browser journey, 0 of 2019 provider-method entries happened while a transaction was open.

SEC-RW4-I4 (the GET session touch waits the busy timeout) stays accepted by dec-mv3vuiqw-01d3c5c9.

Folded as follows:
- SEC-E-1 = TR-E-1 (major);
- SEC-E-2 = TR-E-2 (minor);
- SEC-E-I1..I5 = TR-E-7 (info).

Security finds no way to change an amount, move money twice, reach another customer's payment, forge a webhook, or read a secret or payer data. Its seven class checks pass.

Security's position is approve-with-conditions only if SEC-E-1 is routed by decision before delivery, otherwise changes required. Because the phase is still open and webhooks.js has no later owner, I take the in-phase route: changes requested.

findings.detail:
Deltas 1-4 are each recorded by a decision. I accept them:
- the unmapped type 400, with the runbook subscribing only the seven events;
- the env-only algorithm list, which falls back to the default;
- RS_EXPLAIN_REPHRASE inert;
- test:live-paypal exiting 1 as a whole.

Deltas 5-21 are acceptable additions or interpretations, none of which contradicts a contract:
- 5: 400/403 follow the RBAC matrix;
- 6: the extra redirect outcomes;
- 7: idempotent abandon;
- 8: an upper-case issue only;
- 9: approvalUrl null;
- 10: 'cancelled' after the void rule;
- 11-12: INVALID_STATE and the inline expiry pass;
- 13: accepted by dec-mv3vuigy-0109feb6 with a tested backstop;
- 14-17: placement;
- 18-20: fake and stub interpretations, LIVE-DEPENDENT;
- 21: FU-37.

Unlisted, found by reading:
- 22: PAYMENT.AUTHORIZATION.CREATED also applies from approved and unknown(prev approved). This is an acceptable widening of 'pending -> authorized'; the wider part is TR-E-1.
- 23: reconciler housekeeping does not purge rate_events (spec Reconciler step 9). retention.js does it hourly (24 h, 48 h for request_create), so the effect is equivalent.
- 24: refunds send payment_operations.amount_cents, while create and capture send supplier_orders.total_cents. They are equal by construction (TR-E-5).
- 25: the UI renders no notice for paypal=not_approved or check_failed. The buyer sees the unchanged order with its approval button.

The engineers' mutation proofs (e2 M1-M9, e3 nine, e4 twelve, e6 two) have no ev ids, and e1 reset 2's red-first run is unrecorded.

I reproduced the ones that matter on the final tree, recorded: 17 mutants killed with green controls (ev:ev-mv3xewar-0170b19e). They cover:
- the amount source;
- the capture result fence, the lease guard and the start CAS;
- the takeover exclusion and read-before-resend;
- the claim's AUTHORIZATION_PENDING and the expiry void rule;
- the void rule (local void of an in-flight authorize, a missing provider void);
- the resolution hooks (authorize record, webhook);
- webhook dedupe (over all rows, pre-verify);
- RS-20's sensitivity to a second capture;
- the listener CSP.

This matches e2's M2/M3/M6/M7, e3's dedupe, hook and scoping, e4's lease, fence and takeover, and e6's two controls.

The two survivors are explained (TR-E-4, TR-E-5). The test-after-code gap is closed for this review. The process note stays in the pilot report.
